Rikune Dual-Use Disclosure

Rikune is a dual-use reverse-engineering and security-analysis workbench. It can
inspect binaries, recover program structure, identify suspicious behavior, and
support defensive malware research. The same capabilities could be misused
against systems or software without authorization.

Use Rikune only on samples, systems, and environments that you own or are
explicitly authorized to assess. Users are responsible for complying with
applicable law, license terms, organizational policy, privacy requirements, and
safe malware-handling practices. Do not use Rikune to deploy malware, evade
security controls, obtain unauthorized access, or harm third parties.

Official npm releases are prepared as immutable staged candidates by CI. The
publishable `npm-release-candidate` artifact is exposed only after the exact-tag
stage run has completed its static OCI build, signature, attestation, and
runtime verification. A human maintainer must wait for that entire run to
succeed, verify the artifact manifest's tag, commit, integrity, run URL, and OCI
digest, acknowledge this disclosure, and publish the exact tarball through an
interactive npm ceremony protected by account 2FA. CI is not authorized to hold
an npm publication token or publish the package.

The reviewed ceremony publishes the staged tarball explicitly as the latest
release with `npm publish rikune-1.4.0.tgz --tag latest --access public`; the
maintainer completes npm's interactive 2FA prompt locally and never transmits
the one-time code to CI or an AI assistant.

Security issues should be reported privately through the repository's GitHub
Security Advisories. Do not include live malware, credentials, or sensitive
third-party data in public issues.
